OK, so, from the libgcrypt-1.12.2 source, here's `gcry_check_version()`

```
const char *
gcry_check_version (const char *req_version)
{ 
  return _gcry_check_version (req_version);
}
```

and here's `_gcry_check_version()`:

```
/* If REQ_VERSION is non-NULL, check that the version of the library
   is at minimum the requested one.  Returns the string representation
   of the library version if the condition is satisfied; return NULL
   if the requested version is newer than that of the library.

   If a NULL is passed to this function, no check is done, but the
   string representation of the library is simply returned.  */
const char *
_gcry_check_version (const char *req_version)
{
    const char *ver = VERSION;
    int my_major, my_minor, my_micro;
    int rq_major, rq_minor, rq_micro;
    const char *my_plvl;

    if (req_version && req_version[0] == 1 && req_version[1] == 1)
        return _gcry_compat_identification ();

    /* Initialize library.  */
    global_init ();

    if ( !req_version )
        /* Caller wants our version number.  */
	return ver;

    /* Parse own version number.  */
    my_plvl = parse_version_string( ver, &my_major, &my_minor, &my_micro );
    if ( !my_plvl )
        /* very strange our own version is bogus.  Shouldn't we use
	   assert() here and bail out in case this happens?  -mo.  */
	return NULL;

    /* Parse requested version number.  */
    if (!parse_version_string (req_version, &rq_major, &rq_minor, &rq_micro))
      return NULL;  /* req version string is invalid, this can happen.  */

    /* Compare version numbers.  */
    if ( my_major > rq_major
	|| (my_major == rq_major && my_minor > rq_minor)
	|| (my_major == rq_major && my_minor == rq_minor
	    && my_micro > rq_micro)
	|| (my_major == rq_major && my_minor == rq_minor
                                 && my_micro == rq_micro))
      {
	return ver;
      }

    return NULL;
}
```

Unless I'm missing something, the crash would have to be in `global_init()`.

From the stack trace, the crash appears to be happening in ntdll.dll, so that may well be an NT native system call or something such as that.

> gcry_check_version(NULL) segfaults during start

I'm not so sure of that.

From [the list of NT status codes](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-erref/596a1078-e883-4972-9bbc-49e60bebca55), there's `STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION = 0xC0000005`, for which the error message is "The instruction at 0x%08lx referenced memory at 0x%08lx. The memory could not be %s.", and there's `STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE = 0xC0000008`, for which the error message is `An invalid HANDLE was specified.` The latter is what's occurring here.

It may be that passing an invalid `HANDLE` will, in some cases, cause a crash rather than the Windows equivalent of UN\*X `EINVAL`. (I have some vague memories that V6 UNIX might have caused a `SIGSEGV` rather than an error return if you passed in a null pointer to a system call where the pointer would be dereferenced.)

Without a PDB for libgcrypt, that makes it harder to track down. Google's AI seems to think that Microsoft offers a PDB for ntdll from its public symbol server, but from looking at some of the references it provides, it might have misread it.

@geraldcombs I think I got it, but I'd be happy if you could try and confirm this. I used https://github.com/rainers/cv2pdb to generate the PDB, it worked flawlessly.

0001-Fix-Invalid-Handle-crash-on-Win32.patch:
```
From 5979c49b981142efc2a0a8d67ee65fe62252dad7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gpotter2 <10530980+gpotter2@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 7 May 2026 00:52:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix 'Invalid Handle' crash on Win32

---
 src/hwfeatures.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/hwfeatures.c b/src/hwfeatures.c
index 1b107e63..34017cf7 100644
--- a/src/hwfeatures.c
+++ b/src/hwfeatures.c
@@ -336,7 +336,7 @@ _gcry_get_sysconfdir (void)
               strcat (appdata, "/GNU/etc/gcrypt/");
             }
           xfree (buf);
-          CloseHandle (handle);
+          FreeLibrary(handle);
         }
       if (!appdata)
         appdata = xstrdup ("c:/ProgramData/GNU/etc/gcrypt/");
--
2.47.0.windows.1
```

Btw to rebuild libgcrypt you can use this:

build.sh:
```
# Download libgcrypt
curl "https://gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/libgcrypt/libgcrypt-1.12.2.tar.bz2" | tar xvjf -

# Download libgpg-error
curl "https://gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/libgpg-error/libgpg-error-1.60.tar.bz2" | tar xvjf -

# Create w64root
mkdir w64root
export w64root="$(pwd)/w64root"

# Build libgpg-error
cd libgpg-error-*
./autogen.sh --build-w64 --enable-shared --enable-install-gpg-error-config --enable-static || exit 1
make || exit 1
make install || exit 1
cd ..

# Build libgcrypt
cd libgcrypt-*
cp ../0001-Fix-Invalid-Handle-crash-on-Win32.patch .
git apply 0001-Fix-Invalid-Handle-crash-on-Win32.patch
./autogen.sh --build-w64 --enable-shared --enable-static || exit 1
make || exit 1
make install || exit 1
cd ..
```

It's crashing here:

```
const char *
_gcry_get_sysconfdir (void)
{
#ifdef HAVE_W32_SYSTEM
  static char *appdata;

  if (!appdata)
    {
      HRESULT (WINAPI *func)(HWND,int,HANDLE,DWORD,LPSTR);
      void *handle;
      char *buf;

      handle = LoadLibraryEx ("shell32.dll", NULL, 0);
      if (handle)
        {
          buf = xmalloc (MAX_PATH+17+1); /* Space for "/GNU/etc/gcrypt/" */
          func = (void *)GetProcAddress (handle, "SHGetFolderPathA");
          if (func && func (NULL, CSIDL_COMMON_APPDATA, NULL, 0, buf) >= 0)
            {
              appdata = xmalloc (strlen (buf) + 17 + 1);
              strcpy (appdata, buf);
              strcat (appdata, "/GNU/etc/gcrypt/");
            }
          xfree (buf);
          CloseHandle (handle); <--!!!!!!!!!!!!! HERE !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!-----------
        }
      if (!appdata)
        appdata = xstrdup ("c:/ProgramData/GNU/etc/gcrypt/");
    }

  return appdata;
#else  /*!HAVE_W32_SYSTEM*/
  return "/etc/gcrypt/";
#endif /*!HAVE_W32_SYSTEM*/
}
```

I think it's crashing because you're supposed to use `FreeLibrary` that [checks references](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/libloaderapi/nf-libloaderapi-freelibrary) unlike `CloseHandle` before freeing. Could be that Visual Studio's debugger injects some other usage of shell32.dll somewhere in the process, and because references to libraries are shared by processes it blows up. Also it tracks as [this was introduced](https://github.com/gpg/libgcrypt/commit/d5e3cbfd8845a872d39f468da27a443cea4587e2) in 1.12.0

(Off-topic, but it seems that this libgcrypt commit also introduces the creation of some kind of empty configuration folder that remains after Wireshark uninstallation: ![image](/uploads/07f4286a21d7e2e42afb72c87454e616/image.png){width=400})
